{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-48120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-1 & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ 2(x-1), & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}3x+2, & x<1 \\\\ 4, & x=1 \\\\ -3x+8, & x>1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-1}, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $f(1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x) & ={1}^{3}-1=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x) & =2(1-1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=f(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $g(1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}g(x) & =3(1)+2=5, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}g(x) & =-3(1)+8=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)=5.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}g(x)\\ne g(1)$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=1:$\n\n- $h(1)={e}^{(1-1)}=1$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{(1-1)}=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}h(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 1}h(x)=h(1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo, the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, only $g(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48120" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "27a88aa489df12d166de0c8cb614ad743db8903e379768fae85c8fcecbbaeac1"} {"question" "q-48120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48120" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$g(x)$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the functions $\\,f(x),\\,g(x)\\,$ and $\\,h(x)\\,$ below, which of them has a point discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+3 & x<-1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1 \\\\ 3x+4, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ g(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+1), & x<-1 \\\\ 0, & x=-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-1, & x>-1\\end{aligned} \\\\  \\\\ h(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+1, & x\\le -1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+x+1, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check each function, in turn:\n\nLet's check our continuity conditions for $f(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}f(x) & =2(-1)^{3}+3=1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}f(x) & =3(-1)+4=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$ $\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ It's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-1$ is a point discontinuity. \n\nNow, let's check our continuity conditions for $g(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $g(-1)=0$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}g(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1+1)=0, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}g(x) & =(-1)^{2}-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=0.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}g(x)=g(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nFinally, let's check our continuity conditions for $h(x)$ at $x=-1:$\n\n- $h(-1)=(-1)^{2}+1=2$ exists $✓$\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{-}}h(x) & =(-1)^{2}+1=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,(-1)^{+}}h(x) & =2(-1)^{2}+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left and right-sided limits are equal, we conclude\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=2.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}h(x)=h(-1)$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\nSo,  the function $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, only $f(x)$ has a point discontinuity at $x=-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "27a88aa489df12d166de0c8cb614ad743db8903e379768fae85c8fcecbbaeac1"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$f(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ and $h(x)$ only"] ["text" "$g(x)$ only"] ["text" "$h(x)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-48123" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/d036e94f6a07c526c9609ca2030cc785927562b0771335bbe12aa5fe27c3e369.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=-3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(-3)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-3)}f(x)\\ne f(-3)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=-3$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nA similar argument applies for $x=3,$ which is also a removable discontinuity. \n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=3$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "27a88aa489df12d166de0c8cb614ad743db8903e379768fae85c8fcecbbaeac1"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=-3$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=0$ only"] ["text" "$x=3$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71067" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=-3$ and $x=3$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={\\begin{aligned}4x, & x<2 \\\\ 4, & x=2 \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x>2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f(2)$ exists\n- ${lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)$\n- $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's check our continuity conditions:\n\n- $f(2)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The left and right-sided limits are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}(4x)=8, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}{x}^{2}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that statement II is false and\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x).\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSince the second condition failed, $x=2$ is not a removable discontinuity. So, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "27a88aa489df12d166de0c8cb614ad743db8903e379768fae85c8fcecbbaeac1"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-71180" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/assets/ae097e5f002d970ffe7393cacce708d431f4ef1f46c5a2c95e3fa1f5846d2c7d.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above. For what values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x)$ have a removable discontinuity?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function has a discontinuity at $x=2.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(2)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=0$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)\\ne f(2)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=2$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function also has a discontinuity at $x=4.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(4)=-3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)=-1$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 4}f(x)\\ne f(4)\\,\\times $\n\nSo it's the third condition that failed, which tells us that $x=4$ is a removable discontinuity.\n\nThe function has a discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(1)=3$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=-1$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nFinally, the function has a discontinuity at $x=3.$ Checking our continuity conditions, we have:\n\n- $f(3)=2$ exists $\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to 3}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\times $\n\nSo this time, the second condition failed, and therefore $x=3$ is not a removable discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $x=2$ and $x=4$ only." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "27a88aa489df12d166de0c8cb614ad743db8903e379768fae85c8fcecbbaeac1"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=-1$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ and $x=3$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=4$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"} {"question" "q-104419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=2$ and $x=4$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14070465/state.json" "file_sha256" "232359b450931cfa800694d71437fc79f6de26c14a77abfdda9fddca4dd4a991"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2485 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
